\subsection{RLVR-Aware Variant: Preserving Exploration Stability}
\label{sec:rasp_rlvr}

The Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff (\S\ref{sec:tradeoff}) implies that optimizing for SFT recovery (removing redundancy) is counterproductive for RLVR stability.
We therefore introduce \textbf{\rasp{}-RLVR}, which \emph{inverts} the overlap signal to protect functionally redundant structures that serve as exploration buffers during GRPO training:
\begin{equation}
    S_\text{RLVR}(h) = I(h) \times F(h)^\alpha,
    \label{eq:score_rlvr}
\end{equation}
where high overlap $F(h)$ now \emph{increases} the score, protecting redundant structures from pruning.
The scoring is symmetric to the original \rasp{} (Eq.~\ref{eq:score}): both use the same importance $I(h)$ and overlap $F(h)$ signals computed in Stage~1, differing only in whether overlap increases or decreases the pruning score.

This design is motivated by our diagnostic finding that \rasp{}'s 6$\times$ collapse acceleration (\S\ref{sec:collapse}) is causally attributable to redundancy removal.
\rasp{}-RLVR tests the converse prediction: if preserving redundant structures stabilizes GRPO training, then the inverted scoring should delay or prevent collapse relative to both \rasp{} (which removes redundancy) and Standard pruning (which is overlap-blind).

The per-layer capacity floor (\S\ref{sec:stage2}) applies identically to \rasp{}-RLVR, ensuring comparable architectural constraints across pruning variants.
[TBD-RLVR: Experimental validation in \S\ref{sec:rasp_rlvr_exp}.]

\subsection{Stage 3: Two-Stage Recovery}

The U-shape suggests a phase transition in group selection: below a threshold overlap weight, the CKA signal introduces noise relative to importance-only ranking; above it, overlap-based reranking consistently selects more reasoning-preserving (but RL-destabilizing) pruning configurations.

\subsection{From Diagnosis to Solution: RASP-RLVR}
\label{sec:rasp_rlvr_exp}

The Redundancy-Stability Tradeoff predicts that \emph{inverting} the CKA signal---protecting rather than removing functionally redundant structures---should stabilize GRPO training.
We test this prediction with \rasp{}-RLVR ($S_\text{RLVR}(h) = I(h) \times F(h)^\alpha$; \S\ref{sec:rasp_rlvr}), which preferentially prunes structures with \emph{low} overlap (functionally unique) while preserving high-overlap exploration buffers.

